WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.920
We present Haptic Revolver, a
reconfigurable VR controller that

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.179
enables touch, sheer, texture, and shape
rendering on interactive surfaces.

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:15.960
Existing VR controllers only support
vibrotactile stimulation which is

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.960
limited in its ability to render
different textures and shapes.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.960
What if, as you explored a virtual scene, 
you could feel the surfaces you touched?

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.460
Our device haptically renders the scene by making
contact with your finger when you touch

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.390
the surface and positioning arbitrary
haptic elements in virtual space

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:38.020
allowing you to feel what's beneath your
finger.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:42.820
The haptic revolver consists of a wheel 
that contains various haptic elements.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:47.280
A servo motor moves this wheel up and 
down as you contact a virtual surface.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:53.420
A DC motor spins the wheel to render shear forces
and position haptic elements under the fingertip.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.420
The thumb button on the side of the controller
enables input.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:04.140
Because our device is tracked we can spatially
register haptic elements with the environment.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.420
The motor spins the haptic wheel to ensure
alignment between the features on the wheel

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:13.890
and the virtual scene. Through a
perceptual user evaluation we found that

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.369
we can adjust the wheel speed and
direction to manipulate the size of

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:22.880
virtual objects and even simulate motion
in multiple dimensions.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.680
The haptic wheels are interchangeable and can 
contain different haptic elements such as shapes,

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.000
textures, or active electronic components.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.400
Wheels can also be custom-designed
for different applications.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:42.040
For example, here we show a card table
using a wheel with various textures and edges.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.960
When the user moves from one
surface to another our rendering engine

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.410
positions the wheel so that the
appropriate texture lies beneath the

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:53.700
finger and that the edge is felt in the
correct spot.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:02:00.360
In this scene we show how shapes on the wheel
can add to the realism of a virtual keyboard.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:06.500
A user can even brush along the rows of keys to
feel the grooves. By sensing the force

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:11.200
the finger exerts on the wheel we turn
passive elements into interactive tools.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.239
Here we show how a simple plastic nub
can be used as the tip of a scan of

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:21.870
spray paint. For additional customization
electronic components such as buttons

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:26.910
and joysticks can be placed on the wheel.
In this scene, when the user approaches a

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:30.750
virtual interactive widget, the rendering
engine places an appropriate physical

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:35.160
widget under the fingertip. Here we show
how the user can physically interact

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.080
with virtual knobs, buttons, and switches.

